             credit the +0.9 A. (DS suggested crediting it — overridden: the pre-registration is explicit
             and the A-credit is an architecture judgment, not an executor call.)",

  theorem_vindicated: "The prevention theorem's corollary (H1 NECESSARY for anticipatory H2) is CONFIRMED,
             not contradicted. H2 passed here for a NON-anticipatory reason: with lead=0 the directive fires
             AT excursion onset, so arm_A is a FASTER REACTION (acts at entry) vs arm_B (acts later). The
             35% reduction is reactive-speed, not anticipation. The excursion had already begun when arm_A
             acted. This is exactly the case the theorem separates: reactive prevention ≠ anticipatory
             prevention. No theorem is broken.",

  root_cause: "PROJECTOR DEFECT (not control coupling). Verified against the deployed Markov T (antofagasta):
             at the h=7 alert horizon, P(excursion within 7) ≥ 0.58 from EVERY bin (bin5=0.580, bin4=0.606,
             bin3=0.677) — all >> the 0.15 threshold → the alert is trivially always-on → no discrimination
             → lead=0. h1_bin_bar passed (fires at bin≥3) only because it fires at EVERY bin.",

  the_fix_verified: "SHORT alert horizon discriminates. At h=1: bin5=0.062, bin4=0.098 (both < 0.15 → SILENT),
             bin3=0.228 (> 0.15 → ALERT). So h=1 with the existing 0.15 threshold fires from DRIFTING (bin3)
             and is silent from HEALTHY (bin4/5) → genuine lead (fires before entry to E). This is a fix to
             a PROJECTOR property (discrimination), verifiable against the model BEFORE the re-run — NOT
